A||||6||LAMBO||cooling problem opinions needed||||1||
Z||000000||LAMBO||07-25-2001||02:10 PM||||I've got an 88 v6 5 speed thats giving me some cooling problems, or so the gauge tells me. It supposedly has the hytpertech chip, 160 thermo and low temp fan switch. Here's the scenario. <P>In the morning drive to work (1 hour drive) its cool enough outside that I don't need to run the AC. In this case, I don't seem to have a problem, other than the gauge reads the same temp as other Fieros I've had in the past that didn't have any of the hypertech stuff installed. <P>On the drive home, it's warmed up enough outside that I'm running the AC. As I'm driving home, the temp gauge is slowly rising the whole trip and will get up close to the 220 mark on the gauge (enough to make the check engine light come on). Then, the gauge will start to erratically bounce back and forth between 220 and about 180. Not slowly, but sudden jumps. If I turn the AC off, it seems to help a little, but the temp never comes back down to where it should be normal. The real kicker is that if I pull over to the side of the road and let it just idle for a few minutes, it'll cool off to normal (at least the gauge goes back to normal). Kinda opposite of what you'd normally think.<P>I'm at a loss here guys. Everything seems to be functioning properly. Could there be a slight "clog" in the coolant flow somewhere. Any opinions would be greatly appreciated.<BR>Todd||||reg||1
Z||000001||DaveL||07-25-2001||02:23 PM||||Sounds as though you may have air trapped in the system, need to burp the radiator. Or mybe a sticky thermostat.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Joe Torma||07-25-2001||03:22 PM||||Check for collapsed hoses....don't trust a visual.  They are usually 2 layers and occasionally they will separate and block the flow<P>joe||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||07-25-2001||05:23 PM||||Sounds like you most likly have air in the system. When you turn your AC on, it should turn the cooling fan on no matter what temp the engine is at, see if its doing this. I don't know why everyone thinks 220 is so hot, I run there most every night I race and don't turn my fan on. Ya know, the stock switch doesn't turn the fan on till what? 235? I doubt they would put such a high temp fan switch on there if the engine couldn't take it.<P>Also, bout that check engine light comming on. Thats not normal, even for overheating conditions. Check the codes and see what the light was really on for. <P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 4spd Auto (Currently in repair, wrecked by "Red-Headed Step Child")<BR><I>I live my life a 1/4 mile at a time. Nothing else matters</I>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||07-25-2001||08:53 PM||||If it has an air bubble in it, it could cause the "check engine" light to come on if it trips the "low Coolant" error.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fiero_Freak||07-25-2001||10:51 PM||||     Check the engine code.  I have an 87 with exactly the same setup chip and all.  I just found that my EGR system is screwed up.  Mine ended up being the EGR selinoid.  The egr accually helps the engine stay cooler.  Here's the deal...  To set the code the temp needs to be over 187 and the egr needs to be at 50% and it's not being sensed open.  At highway speed my computer is saying that it's at about 45 percent.  With the A/C on it kicks it up just enough to go over the 50 percent mark.  If all those conditions exist for over 8 seconds it throws a code 32.  Check that and I'd be willing to bet it's your problem.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_Left_01.jpg"> <BR>Two '87 GT's...<BR>   ...Double Trouble||||reg||1
Z||000006||LAMBO||07-25-2001||11:05 PM||||Thanks guys. The first thing I was going to do was burp the system just to rule out an air lock possibility. Yep, the code thing was throwing me a curve too. I'll check to see what code its giving. I've never heard of the EGR thing. I'm always learning something new from this forum every time I'm on here.<BR>Todd <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
